{
 "about": "Shared by the Python test (tests/unit/backend/test_unicode_invisible.py) and the Vitest of the dashboard: the same strings must get the same verdict, count and positions on both sides. Lines count from 1 by LF; a column counts from 1 in CODE POINTS (not UTF-16 units). `ok`: count and where = every marked character [line, column]; `too_many`: more than 50 marked, count only; `refuse`: the first refused character: `code` (\"U+XXXX\", upper-case hex, at least 4 digits), `code_point`, `line` and `column`; line 1 is the first line of the file and a column counts code points. The strings are ASCII-escaped on purpose: nobody can review a character that is not drawn. `emptiness` (a separate list: `cases` keeps its shape): whole skill files, `text` = `header` + `body`, whose verdict is `empty_body` (the body is only whitespace and marked characters), `hidden_characters` (a refused character wins over the cut) or `ok`; the two sides agree because every character on which `str.strip()` and the JS `\\s` differ (U+FEFF, U+001C..U+001F, U+0085) is marked or refused before the cut.",
 "max_marked": 50,
